<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Nicias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">421 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicias">Nicias</a> was a treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> concluded in the spring of 421 BCE, ending the first phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, known as the Archidamian War. Named after the Athenian general Nicias who negotiated it, it was meant to last fifty years and provided for the exchange of prisoners and captured places, but several allies refused it, and open war resumed by 414 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 425 BCE the Spartans lost the battles of Pylos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sphacteria">Sphacteria</a>, and the Athenians held 292 prisoners, at least 120 of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a>. In 424 BCE the Spartan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasidas">Brasidas</a> captured Amphipolis, but the Athenians suffered a major defeat at Delium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. In 422 BCE they were beaten again at Amphipolis, where both Brasidas and the Athenian politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleon">Cleon</a> were killed. Both sides were by then exhausted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> feared that its allies might revolt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> was shaken by its losses and by the failure of its yearly invasions.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> also faced a problem with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>. Its thirty-year peace with Argos was about to expire, and the Argives would not renew it unless the border region of Cynuria was returned to them. The Spartans judged it hard to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Argos at the same time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The negotiations were started by Pleistoanax, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicias">Nicias</a>. Most places were to return to their prewar state, except that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> kept Nisaea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. Pylos was to go back to Sparta, Amphipolis to Athens, and the prisoners were to be exchanged. Temples were to be open to worshippers from all cities, and the oracle at Delphi was to regain its autonomy. Athens would go on collecting tribute from the states that had paid it since the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a> but could not force them to become allies, and it agreed to help Sparta if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> revolted. Six cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argilus">Argilus</a>, Stagirus, Acanthus, Stolus, Olynthus and Spartolus, were listed as neutral.</p><p>Seventeen representatives from each side swore an oath to keep the treaty for fifty years. The Spartans included the kings Pleistoanax and Agis II, and the Athenians included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicias">Nicias</a>, Laches, Lamachus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes-general">Demosthenes</a>. Athens's chief goal, the return of Amphipolis, was blocked when Clearidas obtained a clause in the treaty cancelling the transfer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Few of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s allies agreed to the peace; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, Corinth, Elis, Megara and Amphipolis opposed it. Boeotia refused to return Athenian captives and the fortress of Panactum, while Corinth's quarrels over Potidaea, Corcyra and its lost territory in the north-west were unresolved. Megara had lost its port of Nisaea, and Elis resented Sparta's conduct. Most of the terms were ignored or only partly carried out. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> the treaty lasted about six years and ten months, during which the two sides attacked each other's territory, and many historians blame militant factions that gained power in both cities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peace of Nicias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
